# TLS server, CHUNKING reception with TLS shutdown mid-chunk
gnutls
#
# The client opens a chunk of 88 bytes, sends only 20 of them, and then shuts
# the TLS session down while leaving the TCP connection open.  That leaves the
# server in the middle of a BDAT command with 68 bytes still outstanding, so its
# body reader is still dispatching through the readers installed for the TLS
# session at the moment that session, and the transfer buffer those readers work
# on, are released.
#
# Reading has to stop there.  Going on would either reach into the released
# buffer or - just as bad - read the remainder of the connection in clear, on
# the socket the peer negotiated encryption for.  So what is expected below is
# the lost-connection response and then end of file: no further command may be
# taken in clear, and the daemon must not be left reading for the rest of the
# chunk until the receive timeout expires.
#
exim -DSERVER=server -DSRV=tls -bd -oX PORT_D
****
#
# Chunk arithmetic: 88 declared, 20 sent.  "To: Susan@random.com" is 20 bytes and
# the ">>> " prefix suppresses the trailing CRLF, so 68 bytes of the chunk never
# arrive and the header line is left unterminated.  Stopping short of the line
# ending matters: a chunk whose first header line does end, but with a bare LF,
# is refused outright with "552 Message header not CRLF terminated" as soon as
# that line is complete, which would answer a question about header syntax
# instead of the one this test asks.
#
client-anytls 127.0.0.1 PORT_D
??? 220
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250-STARTTLS
??? 250 HELP
STARTTLS
??? 220
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250 HELP
MAIL FROM:<someone@some.domain>
??? 250
RCPT TO:<CALLER@test.ex>
??? 250
BDAT 88 LAST
>>> To: Susan@random.com
stoptls
??? 421
???*
****
millisleep 500
killdaemon
no_msglog_check
